<speak>
<p>We want to turn h of Z and P into polynomials in P while preserving its norm identity. Replace only Z, and keep P unchanged. The substitutions one, negative one, and i are fourth roots of unity: each satisfies Z to the fourth equals one. They therefore respect the four cycle relation and define ring maps.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Recall the decomposition into H zero, plus Z times H one, plus Z squared times H two, plus Z cubed times H three. Every H is still a polynomial in P. At Z equal to one, the four weights are one, one, one, one. We get the sum of all four H polynomials.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>At Z equal to negative one, the weights become one, negative one, one, negative one. Thus h of negative one and P is H zero minus H one plus H two minus H three. Both of these first two evaluated polynomials have integer coefficients.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>At Z equal to i, the weights are one, i, negative one, negative i. Collect the real terms as H zero minus H two, and the imaginary terms as i times the difference H one minus H three. This evaluated polynomial has Gaussian integer coefficients.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>We could also substitute negative i. This conjugates the coefficients of h of i and P, while leaving P unchanged. Its polynomial is already determined. Coefficient conjugation here is different from star: for Gaussian coefficients, star conjugates the coefficients and also reverses the powers of P.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Let epsilon be one of our three chosen roots. Follow one term with real coefficient h sub a b: h sub a b times Z to the a times P to the b. First evaluate Z at epsilon. The term becomes h sub a b times epsilon to the a times P to the b. Now take star: conjugate epsilon and reverse the power of P. We obtain h sub a b times conjugate epsilon to the a times P to the negative b.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Follow the other path. First take star in the original two variable ring: the term becomes h sub a b times Z to the negative a times P to the negative b. Then evaluate Z at epsilon. The result is h sub a b times epsilon to the negative a times P to the negative b. The endpoints agree, because conjugate epsilon equals epsilon inverse on the unit circle. Applying this term by term proves that evaluation commutes with star.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Evaluation preserves multiplication and star. Therefore h of Z and P times its star equals four u squared becomes h of epsilon and P times its own star equals four u squared. The relation P to the u squared equals one is still in force. All three evaluated polynomials retain the same norm.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>For u equal to three, that norm is still thirty six. Once the parity check permits division by two in the coefficient ring, halving both factors will divide the norm by four, giving nine. That normalization is the next step. Here we have proved that these evaluations preserve the norm, with P unchanged.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
